# The provisioning Job's credentials, as a template that is
# deliberately not applicable, for the reason `secret.yaml.example`
# beside it gives at length.
#
# These are not the server's credentials and this Secret is not the
# server's Secret. Creating roles and schemas is an administrative right
# the serving pod never has: deployment.yaml references no key of this
# object, and the agreement test asserts that it does not.
#
# It exists for the length of the provisioning transaction. Delete it
# when the Job has completed, and make it again for the next upgrade's
# rerun. Idempotently, because the init transaction is rerunnable and a
# retry after a failed Job would otherwise die on AlreadyExists before
# it reached the thing that actually failed:
#
#     kubectl create secret generic vinga-init \
#       --from-literal=ADMIN_URL="$ADMIN_URL" \
#       --from-literal=VINGA_DB_USER=vinga \
#       --from-literal=VINGA_DB_RO_PASSWORD="$VINGA_DB_RO_PASSWORD" \
#       --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
#
# Both values come from variables rather than being written into the
# command, and `VINGA_DB_RO_PASSWORD` in particular is generated ONCE
# and kept wherever this deployment keeps secrets. Generating it inline
# on each rerun looks tidy and is a bug: the SQL runs `ALTER ROLE ...
# WITH LOGIN PASSWORD` on every run, so a fresh value rotates the
# analyst role's password on every upgrade and silently invalidates
# whatever credential is in use. Generate it with
# `openssl rand -hex 32` the first time and pass the same value after
# that.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: vinga-init
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: vinga
type: Opaque
stringData:
  # The administrative connection the SQL is run over: a role that may
  # create roles and create schemas in this database, which is a
  # superuser or the database's owner with CREATEROLE. It names the
  # database to provision, so a typo here provisions the wrong one.
  ADMIN_URL: REPLACE-ME-with-postgresql-admin-connection-url
  # Which role the three schemas are created WITH AUTHORIZATION to. The
  # same value as deployment.yaml's VINGA_DB_USER, and it has to be:
  # `CREATE` on the database does not grant the server role `CREATE`
  # inside a schema somebody else owns, which is where Alembic would
  # then fail to make its tables.
  VINGA_DB_USER: vinga
  # The read-only analyst role's password, required rather than
  # defaulted. `vinga_ro` can read every recorded conversation, and the
  # SQL falls back to the public string of the same name when this is
  # not in the environment, so an omission installs a well-known
  # password rather than failing.
  #
  # The same value on every rerun, per the header: the SQL rotates this
  # role's password each time it runs, so a value generated afresh here
  # is a credential invalidated on every upgrade.
  VINGA_DB_RO_PASSWORD: REPLACE-ME-with-openssl-rand-hex-32-generated-once
